comments.body:
@seelabs, my unit tests will be a source on on-going irritation for you :smile:.

1. Would it help if I divided the test into two sections?  I'll make a run at separating the gw1 -> alice -> gw2 section from the alice -> gw1 -> bob section.

2. I struggled a lot getting this test to work and to verify that the failures were for the reasons I expected.  When I struggle I'm likely to add more comments.  For example,
```
// Pay alice so there is something to ripple.
env (pay (gw1, alice, USD1 (10)));
```
If the comment only said "pay alice", I'd agree with you.  But it turns out that unless you pay alice first, no rippling will occur.  This may seem obvious to you, but it was not obvious to me when I started work on the test.  I will remove some of the comments you feel are superfluous, but you will still feel there are redundant comments.  The code says what the code does.  The comment says what I _intend_ for the code to do (which it might not actually do), and hopefully why.

3. Try an experiment.  Comment out this `close()`:
```
            // Pay alice so there is something to ripple.
            env (pay (gw1, alice, USD1 (10)));
//            env.close();
```
You'll see that there are now 8 failures in the test.  My best guess for the reason for the failure is that the order of the `pay` transactions on lines 319 and 333 was swapped during the `close()` on line 334.  Note also that there is only a 50% likelihood that removing the `close()` will cause that problem.  It's a question of the ordering between account `gw1` and `gw2`.  If you change the account numbers you may get a different ordering.

During a `close` the order of transaction execution is determined solely by the a) account, b) transaction sequence, and c) in unusual cases the transaction ID : https://github.com/ripple/rippled/blob/develop/src/ripple/app/misc/CanonicalTXSet.cpp#L26-L37.  So if transactions are not to the same account, then they may be reordered during the `close()`.

This is why, if there are any `close()` calls, then there must be lots of `close` calls to be sure that the code can be read sensibly from top to bottom.

You may argue that I shouldn't use the `close` calls at all.  There are many unit tests like that.  My preference is to use `close`, because that keeps the situation closer to what happens on the network.  But if enough people voice support for `close`-less unit tests I could be talked into omitting the `close` calls except for cases where a `close` is absolutely required.  But, personally, I think that would be the wrong choice.

reviews.body:
:+1:

Approved; However, here's three things to think about (two that we've talked about before, but I still want to bring them up)

1) Each section of the test is not independent. I'd rather see the test where there's a setup of the needed state and then the test. As written it runs a test then modifies state then runs another tests. It's easier to follow a tests if I don't have to walk through all that came before. Of course if there is common setup it can be factored into a function or lambda.

2) Comments that say what one line of code does when the line of code is already clear clutters up the code. There isn't just one or two of there, in this test almost all the comments are of this form. Some examples:
```
        // Test without featureDepositAuth
        actualTests (supported_amendments() - featureDepositAuth);

        // Test with featureDepositAuth.
        actualTests (supported_amendments() | featureDepositAuth);
```
```
            // gw1 disables rippling to alice and to bob.
            env (trust (gw1, alice["USD"](1000), tfSetNoRipple));
            env (trust (gw1,   bob["USD"](1000), tfSetNoRipple));
```
```
            // Pay alice so there is something to ripple.
            env (pay (gw1, alice, USD1 (10)));
```
If you remove these comments the code would be improved.

3) Adding a `close` after every transactions. I know why you're doing this, but the code would be improved if you only added the close when needed.
